+<h4>11.6 Reading the result &mdash; and correcting an over-claim</h4>
+<div class="callout ok">
+<h4 style="margin-top:0">What the key-expansion run genuinely proves</h4>
+<ul>
+<li>The routine <b>returns normally</b> after 89,148 instructions &mdash; it is not dead code and it does not need a live JNI environment to run its crypto path.</li>
+<li>It reads the <b>genuine AES forward S-box 112 times</b> and <b>rcon exactly 14 times</b>. Fourteen rcon reads is the AES-256 count (AES-128 uses 10, AES-192 uses 12), and it validates <code>w3 == w4 == 0x20</code> = a 256-bit length.</li>
+<li><b>The S-box reads have a clean period-14 structure.</b> An instruction-level histogram (<code>work/unicorn_round_structure.py</code> &rarr; <code>out/42</code>) shows the 112 reads come from exactly <b>8 distinct instructions, each hit exactly 14 times</b> &mdash; two clusters of four (<code>0x3283c/40/58/88</code> and <code>0x33840/44/48/c8</code>). Four S-box lookups &times; 14 rounds is precisely AES-256 <code>SubWord</code> inside the key schedule. This is the single strongest structural signal that the expansion is AES-256.</li>
+<li>It writes a <b>240-byte schedule</b> = 15 &times; 16 bytes, exactly the AES-256 round-key size.</li>
+<li>So the library <b>contains and uses</b> the real AES S-box, inverse S-box and rcon, and the <i>key expansion</i> is structurally AES-256. That much is solid.</li>
+</ul>
+</div>
+<div class="callout bad">
+<h4 style="margin-top:0">Correction: the <u>block ciphers</u> are not validated &mdash; and neither extreme claim is warranted</h4>
+<p>An earlier revision called the whole thing &ldquo;proven AES-256&rdquo; and said the schedule &ldquo;obeys the FIPS-197 recurrence exactly&rdquo;. A functional test (<code>work/unicorn_verify_cipher.py</code>, <code>work/unicorn_roundtrip.py</code>) forced a correction &mdash; but the honest position is <b>inconclusive</b>, not the opposite extreme. What the tests actually show:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><b>The captured 240-byte output is not a standard schedule.</b> Checked word-by-word it breaks the strict FIPS-197 recurrence at <code>w[8]</code> (<code>4c3ce189</code> produced vs <code>e539061e</code> required). But this output sits at <code>ctx+0x30</code>, while the routine's own schedule pointers <code>ctx+0x438</code>/<code>0x458</code> were left pointing at <i>zeroed</i> buffers &mdash; so <code>ctx+0x30</code> may be scratch, and the keyexp almost certainly never ran against a faithful <code>ctx</code> either.</li>
+<li><b>The encrypt/decrypt blocks were never run faithfully.</b> On a zeroed schedule the encrypt block <code>0x2fdcc</code> returns early after 23,186 instructions having read the S-box only <b>32 times</b> (4 instructions &times; 8) &mdash; a truncated path, not a 14-round SubBytes (~160 reads). When the schedule buffers are <i>populated</i>, the same function <b>does not terminate</b>: it hits the 3,000,000-instruction cap with 0 S-box reads, because the OLLVM control-flow-flattened dispatcher never reaches an exit state without the exact C++ object layout. Neither run is the real cipher.</li>
+<li><b>The round-trip &ldquo;failure&rdquo; is therefore not evidence of a non-AES cipher.</b> <code>decrypt(encrypt(pt)) &ne; pt</code> only because <i>both</i> calls took truncated early-exit paths on a zeroed schedule &mdash; it does not show the functions are non-inverse in a faithful run.</li>
+</ul>
+<p><b>Honest conclusion.</b> The AES tables are real, genuinely referenced and genuinely read; the <b>key expansion is structurally AES-256</b> (period-14 SubWord over the true S-box, 14 rcon reads, 240-byte output). The <b>block ciphers could not be validated under emulation</b> because faithfully driving them needs the precise <code>ctx</code> object layout (key string, IV, mode, GCM/CTR counter) that a standalone Unicorn call does not reproduce &mdash; the flattened dispatcher either early-exits or spins. So the correct claim is &ldquo;an AES-table cipher with an AES-256-shaped key expansion&rdquo;, <b>not</b> &ldquo;proven standard AES-256&rdquo; and <b>not</b> &ldquo;proven not-AES&rdquo;. <code>09_native_aes_dump.js</code> settles it on a device by capturing the schedule and I/O the running app actually uses and testing them against a reference AES.</p>
+</div>
