+<section id="findings">
+<h2>16. Detailed findings</h2>
+<p class="muted">73 findings &middot; each with mechanism, impact, evidence, proof-of-concept script and remediation.</p>
+<div id="vulnList">
+
+<article class="vuln card" id="BIZ-01" data-sev="Critical" data-cat="Business logic" data-search="biz-01 coin reward flag get_coin is asserted by the client business logic when a task finishes, ha.c.onready(jsonobject) sets get_coin to &quot;true&quot; if and only if the in-memory instagram response object reports getstatus().equals(&quot;ok&quot;) , then posts the claim to order/syncorder.php . the status is a client-side object; forcing it to &quot;ok&quot; makes the client claim payment for an action that never happened or that instagram rejected. unbounded coin generation. coins are the currency spent on followers and likes, so this converts directly into free engagement for any account the attacker names the core fraud of the entire service. ha.c.java:78 p9.addproperty(&quot;get_coin&quot;, v1_16) with v1_16 = (resp.getstatus()==null || !resp.getstatus().equals(&quot;ok&quot;)) ? &quot;false&quot; : &quot;true&quot; determine the reward exclusively server-side, from an independently fetched instagram state plus a server-issued task nonce. never accept a client-declared success flag.">
+  <header>
+    <div class="vid">BIZ-01</div>
+    <span class="badge" style="--c:#ff3b6b">Critical</span>
+    <span class="cat">Business logic</span>
+  </header>
+  <h3>Coin reward flag <code>get_coin</code> is asserted by the client</h3>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div><h4>How it works</h4><p>When a task finishes, <code>ha.c.onReady(JsonObject)</code> sets <code>get_coin</code> to <code>"true"</code> if and only if the in-memory Instagram response object reports <code>getStatus().equals("ok")</code>, then POSTs the claim to <code>order/syncOrder.php</code>. The status is a client-side object; forcing it to <code>"ok"</code> makes the client claim payment for an action that never happened or that Instagram rejected.</p></div>
+    <div><h4>Impact</h4><p>Unbounded coin generation. Coins are the currency spent on followers and likes, so this converts directly into free engagement for any account the attacker names &mdash; the core fraud of the entire service.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h4>Evidence</h4>
+  <p class="ev"><code>ha.c.java:78</code> <code>p9.addProperty("get_coin", v1_16)</code> with <code>v1_16 = (resp.getStatus()==null || !resp.getStatus().equals("ok")) ? "false" : "true"</code></p>
+  <div class="rowend">
+    <div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><div class="chips"><a class="chip" href="#script-06_task_verification_bypass">06_task_verification_bypass.js</a></div></div>
+    <div class="fixcol"><h4>Remediation</h4><p>Determine the reward exclusively server-side, from an independently fetched Instagram state plus a server-issued task nonce. Never accept a client-declared success flag.</p></div>
+  </div>
+</article>
+
+<article class="vuln card" id="BIZ-02" data-sev="Critical" data-cat="Business logic" data-search="biz-02 reward amount order_value travels from the client business logic the same claim body carries order_value = order.getorder_value() , taken from the local order object, and type = order.getorder_type() . the client therefore states how much the completed task is worth. a hook on order.getorder_value() rewrites the amount before the request is even built. direct monetary inflation: the attacker chooses the payout. even if the server re-derives the amount, the field's presence shows the client is trusted with pricing on the money path. ha.c.java:69 p9.addproperty(&quot;order_value&quot;, v4_8) , :62 addproperty(&quot;type&quot;, v1_11.getorder_type()) ; models.order.getorder_value() look up the reward from the server's own copy of the order keyed by order_id ; ignore any client-supplied value.">
+  <header>
+    <div class="vid">BIZ-02</div>
+    <span class="badge" style="--c:#ff3b6b">Critical</span>
+    <span class="cat">Business logic</span>
+  </header>
+  <h3>Reward amount <code>order_value</code> travels from the client</h3>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div><h4>How it works</h4><p>The same claim body carries <code>order_value = order.getOrder_value()</code>, taken from the local <code>Order</code> object, and <code>type = order.getOrder_type()</code>. The client therefore states how much the completed task is worth. A hook on <code>Order.getOrder_value()</code> rewrites the amount before the request is even built.</p></div>
+    <div><h4>Impact</h4><p>Direct monetary inflation: the attacker chooses the payout. Even if the server re-derives the amount, the field's presence shows the client is trusted with pricing on the money path.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h4>Evidence</h4>
+  <p class="ev"><code>ha.c.java:69</code> <code>p9.addProperty("order_value", v4_8)</code>, <code>:62</code> <code>addProperty("type", v1_11.getOrder_type())</code>; <code>models.Order.getOrder_value()</code></p>
+  <div class="rowend">
+    <div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><div class="chips"><a class="chip" href="#script-06_task_verification_bypass">06_task_verification_bypass.js</a></div></div>
+    <div class="fixcol"><h4>Remediation</h4><p>Look up the reward from the server's own copy of the order keyed by <code>order_id</code>; ignore any client-supplied value.</p></div>
+  </div>
+</article>
+
+<article class="vuln card" id="CRED-01" data-sev="Critical" data-cat="Credentials" data-search="cred-01 instagram account password persisted on-device in a reversible cipher credentials the private-api login path stores the victim's real instagram password in the room table instagram_accounts , column u_w . the value passed to setu_w() is com.bumptech.glide.d.q(password) a keyless static byte transform, not encryption. any process or backup that can read the app's data directory recovers the password in one line. full instagram account takeover. the password (not just a session) is captured, so an attacker can also reset 2fa, change the e-mail and permanently lock the victim out. every account the user ever adds is exposed. ia.v.a(ia.v,string) v7_1.setu_w(com.bumptech.glide.d.q(v6.getpassword())) ; room ddl instagram_accounts(... `u_w` text ...) never accept or store the user's instagram password. use instagram's official oauth (which this app does not); at absolute minimum store only a short-lived session token in android keystore-backed encryptedsharedpreferences.">
+  <header>
+    <div class="vid">CRED-01</div>
+    <span class="badge" style="--c:#ff3b6b">Critical</span>
+    <span class="cat">Credentials</span>
+  </header>
+  <h3>Instagram account PASSWORD persisted on-device in a reversible cipher</h3>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div><h4>How it works</h4><p>The private-API login path stores the victim's real Instagram password in the Room table <code>instagram_accounts</code>, column <code>u_w</code>. The value passed to <code>setU_w()</code> is <code>com.bumptech.glide.d.q(password)</code> &mdash; a keyless static byte transform, not encryption. Any process or backup that can read the app's data directory recovers the password in one line.</p></div>
+    <div><h4>Impact</h4><p>Full Instagram account takeover. The password (not just a session) is captured, so an attacker can also reset 2FA, change the e-mail and permanently lock the victim out. Every account the user ever adds is exposed.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h4>Evidence</h4>
+  <p class="ev"><code>ia.v.a(ia.v,String)</code> &rarr; <code>v7_1.setU_w(com.bumptech.glide.d.q(v6.getPassword()))</code>; Room DDL <code>instagram_accounts(... `u_w` TEXT ...)</code></p>
+  <div class="rowend">
+    <div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><div class="chips"><a class="chip" href="#script-04_credential_theft">04_credential_theft.js</a></div></div>
+    <div class="fixcol"><h4>Remediation</h4><p>Never accept or store the user's Instagram password. Use Instagram's official OAuth (which this app does not); at absolute minimum store only a short-lived session token in Android Keystore-backed EncryptedSharedPreferences.</p></div>
+  </div>
+</article>
+
+<article class="vuln card" id="CRED-02" data-sev="Critical" data-cat="Credentials" data-search="cred-02 2fa seed / backup codes stored in the two_factors table credentials a second room table two_factors holds u_n (username), u_p (password) and s_k (shared secret). c2.t issues the raw query select * from two_factors and populates twofactoraccount.setu_p() . s_k is the totp seed used by account/getsecretkey.php / requestdigitcode.php , and ha.c sends an s_k_2fa field to the backend. defeats two-factor authentication entirely: with the seed an attacker generates valid 6-digit codes forever. password (cred-01) plus this seed is a complete, permanent compromise. c2.t:627 s(&quot;select * from two_factors&quot;) , :660 setu_p(...) ; models.twofactoraccount.gets_k()/getu_p() ; addproperty(&quot;s_k_2fa&quot;, ...) never store a totp seed. if 2fa recovery is genuinely required, wrap it in an androidkeystore aes-gcm key bound to user authentication with hardware attestation.">
+  <header>
+    <div class="vid">CRED-02</div>
+    <span class="badge" style="--c:#ff3b6b">Critical</span>
+    <span class="cat">Credentials</span>
+  </header>
+  <h3>2FA seed / backup codes stored in the <code>two_factors</code> table</h3>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div><h4>How it works</h4><p>A second Room table <code>two_factors</code> holds <code>u_n</code> (username), <code>u_p</code> (password) and <code>s_k</code> (shared secret). <code>c2.t</code> issues the raw query <code>select * from two_factors</code> and populates <code>TwoFactorAccount.setU_p()</code>. <code>s_k</code> is the TOTP seed used by <code>account/getSecretKey.php</code> / <code>requestDigitCode.php</code>, and <code>ha.c</code> sends an <code>s_k_2fa</code> field to the backend.</p></div>
+    <div><h4>Impact</h4><p>Defeats two-factor authentication entirely: with the seed an attacker generates valid 6-digit codes forever. Password (CRED-01) plus this seed is a complete, permanent compromise.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h4>Evidence</h4>
+  <p class="ev"><code>c2.t:627</code> <code>S("select * from two_factors")</code>, <code>:660 setU_p(...)</code>; <code>models.TwoFactorAccount.getS_k()/getU_p()</code>; <code>addProperty("s_k_2fa", ...)</code></p>
+  <div class="rowend">
+    <div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><div class="chips"><a class="chip" href="#script-04_credential_theft">04_credential_theft.js</a></div></div>
+    <div class="fixcol"><h4>Remediation</h4><p>Never store a TOTP seed. If 2FA recovery is genuinely required, wrap it in an AndroidKeyStore AES-GCM key bound to user authentication with hardware attestation.</p></div>
+  </div>
+</article>
+
+<article class="vuln card" id="CRED-03" data-sev="Critical" data-cat="Credentials" data-search="cred-03 webview cookie harvester synthesises an oauth bearer token from sessionid credentials oa.l1.onpagefinished() reads cookiemanager.getcookie(&quot;https://www.instagram.com/&quot;) , extracts sessionid , ds_user_id and mid , then builds &quot;bearer igt:2:&quot; + base64({&quot;ds_user_id&quot;:..,&quot;sessionid&quot;:..}) and writes it to instagram_accounts.u_a . this is silent session-token theft performed on any page load, with no user gesture or confirmation. account takeover without ever learning the password. the synthesised bearer is exactly what instagram's own mobile clients use, so it is fully functional for follow / like / comment / dm automation. oa.l1.java y(cookie,&quot;sessionid&quot;) , y(cookie,&quot;ds_user_id&quot;) , &quot;bearer igt:2:&quot; + base64.encodetostring(...) , v13_3.setu_a(com.bumptech.glide.d.q(v8_9)) do not harvest cookies from a webview. use official instagram oauth with pkce and store only the returned, scoped, expiring access token.">
+  <header>
+    <div class="vid">CRED-03</div>
+    <span class="badge" style="--c:#ff3b6b">Critical</span>
+    <span class="cat">Credentials</span>
+  </header>
+  <h3>WebView cookie harvester synthesises an OAuth bearer token from <code>sessionid</code></h3>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div><h4>How it works</h4><p><code>oa.l1.onPageFinished()</code> reads <code>CookieManager.getCookie("https://www.instagram.com/")</code>, extracts <code>sessionid</code>, <code>ds_user_id</code> and <code>mid</code>, then builds <code>"Bearer IGT:2:" + Base64({"ds_user_id":..,"sessionid":..})</code> and writes it to <code>instagram_accounts.u_a</code>. This is silent session-token theft performed on any page load, with no user gesture or confirmation.</p></div>
+    <div><h4>Impact</h4><p>Account takeover without ever learning the password. The synthesised bearer is exactly what Instagram's own mobile clients use, so it is fully functional for follow / like / comment / DM automation.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h4>Evidence</h4>
+  <p class="ev"><code>oa.l1.java</code> &mdash; <code>y(cookie,"sessionid")</code>, <code>y(cookie,"ds_user_id")</code>, <code>"Bearer IGT:2:" + Base64.encodeToString(...)</code>, <code>v13_3.setU_a(com.bumptech.glide.d.q(v8_9))</code></p>
