ent decrypt(encrypt(pt))&amp;ne;pt is a consequence of both calls taking truncated paths, not proof of a non-aes cipher. so the correct claim is &amp;ldquo;an aes-table cipher with an aes-256-shaped key expansion&amp;rdquo;, not &amp;ldquo;proven standard aes-256&amp;rdquo; and not &amp;ldquo;proven not-aes&amp;rdquo;. separately, none of the 22 registernatives methods has a ([b)[b signature , and no com.nivaroid.topfollow method anywhere in the dex has that descriptor, so whatever this cipher is, it is not directly callable from java as a byte-array primitive. a table-based block cipher built on the real aes s-box is compiled into the shipping binary and used internally by the native request/signature pipeline, but it is invisible to any java-level audit, to the app's own developers reviewing the java layer, and to any tester who only instruments java. crypto that cannot be reached by the documented interface cannot be reviewed, rotated, disabled or tested it is an unauditable primitive sitting in the trust path of every signed request. that even its exact algorithm resists validation without the live object layout makes it less reviewable still: an analyst cannot safely assume textbook aes semantics. it also means an analyst who only examines the dex will conclude, wrongly, that the app performs no native encryption at all. s-box/inverse/rcon located and verified in all three abis ( work/out/30_aes_forensics_arm64.txt ); five referencing functions and the 22-ancestor call graph ( work/out/32_aes_xref_arm64-v8a.json ); unicorn key-expansion log, 112 s-box + 14 rcon reads ( work/out/33_unicorn_aes_arm64.txt ); period-14 s-box-read ip histogram for keyexp and the 4&amp;times;8 encrypt pattern ( work/out/42_round_structure.txt ); block-cipher runs that early-exit / spin, showing they are unvalidated ( work/out/40_verify_cipher.txt , work/out/41_roundtrip.txt ); 22 jni signatures from the dex, none ([b)[b ( work/out/39_jni_full_map.json ) do not ship cryptographic prim