+<article class="vuln card" id="INTE-13" data-sev="Medium" data-cat="Integrity" data-search="inte-13 ecdsa device-attestation key uses a fixed alias and a 25-year self-signed certificate integrity com.nivaroid.topfollow.helper.t performs the app's device attestation against the androidkeystore under the hardcoded alias top_key_4286 . t.o(string) loads the keystore, fetches getcertificate(&quot;top_key_4286&quot;) and getcertificatechain(&quot;top_key_4286&quot;) , signs the input with sha256withecdsa using getkey(&quot;top_key_4286&quot;, null) , and returns base64( signature + &quot;#&quot; + base64(pubkey.getencoded()) + &quot;#&quot; + jsonarray(base64(cert[i].getencoded())) ) . t.sd(string) returns just the signature. the native layer drives this through q.i(jsonobject,string) / x00120b1e , and d3.d.java:738-740 generates the ec key pair and reads ecpublickey.getw() . the alias is a compile-time constant identical on every installation. a fixed, guessable keystore alias means the attestation key is addressable by any code in the process, and the attestation is self-asserted: the app sends its own public key and its own certificate chain alongside the signature, so the server learns the key from the same untrusted channel it uses to verify it. because the certificate chain is generated on-device rather than issued by a hardware attestation root, nothing binds the key to genuine strongbox or tee-backed storage a repackaged build, an emulator or a rooted device can mint an identical-looking chain. the 25-year validity of the signing certificate elsewhere in this app ( 2023-12-15 to 2048-12-08 ) shows the project's posture toward key lifetimes. com.nivaroid.topfollow.helper.t.o()/sd() ; alias literal top_key_4286 ; d3.d.java:738-740 keypairgenerator.getinstance(&quot;ec&quot;) + ecpublickey.getw() ; native driver q.i = x00120b1e use hardware-backed key attestation: generate the key with setisstrongboxbacked(true) and an attestation challenge issued by the server, then verify the certificate chain on the server up to the google hardware attestation root and check attestationsecuritylevel . derive the alias per-installation.">
+  <header>
+    <div class="vid">INTE-13</div>
+    <span class="badge" style="--c:#ffd23d">Medium</span>
+    <span class="cat">Integrity</span>
+  </header>
+  <h3>ECDSA device-attestation key uses a fixed alias and a 25-year self-signed certificate</h3>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div><h4>How it works</h4><p><code>com.nivaroid.topfollow.helper.T</code> performs the app's device attestation against the AndroidKeyStore under the hardcoded alias <code>top_key_4286</code>. <code>T.o(String)</code> loads the keystore, fetches <code>getCertificate("top_key_4286")</code> and <code>getCertificateChain("top_key_4286")</code>, signs the input with <code>SHA256withECDSA</code> using <code>getKey("top_key_4286", null)</code>, and returns <code>Base64( signature + "#" + Base64(pubkey.getEncoded()) + "#" + JSONArray(Base64(cert[i].getEncoded())) )</code>. <code>T.sd(String)</code> returns just the signature. The native layer drives this through <code>q.i(JsonObject,String)</code> / <code>x00120b1e</code>, and <code>d3.d.java:738-740</code> generates the EC key pair and reads <code>ECPublicKey.getW()</code>. The alias is a compile-time constant identical on every installation.</p></div>
+    <div><h4>Impact</h4><p>A fixed, guessable keystore alias means the attestation key is addressable by any code in the process, and the attestation is self-asserted: the app sends its own public key and its own certificate chain alongside the signature, so the server learns the key from the same untrusted channel it uses to verify it. Because the certificate chain is generated on-device rather than issued by a hardware attestation root, nothing binds the key to genuine StrongBox or TEE-backed storage &mdash; a repackaged build, an emulator or a rooted device can mint an identical-looking chain. The 25-year validity of the signing certificate elsewhere in this app (<code>2023-12-15</code> to <code>2048-12-08</code>) shows the project's posture toward key lifetimes.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h4>Evidence</h4>
+  <p class="ev"><code>com.nivaroid.topfollow.helper.T.o()/sd()</code>; alias literal <code>top_key_4286</code>; <code>d3.d.java:738-740</code> <code>KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC")</code> + <code>ECPublicKey.getW()</code>; native driver <code>q.i</code> = <code>x00120b1e</code></p>
+  <div class="rowend">
+    <div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><div class="chips"><a class="chip" href="#script-10_java_crypto_layer">10_java_crypto_layer.js</a> <a class="chip" href="#script-08_backend_traffic_and_servercheck">08_backend_traffic_and_servercheck.js</a></div></div>
+    <div class="fixcol"><h4>Remediation</h4><p>Use hardware-backed key attestation: generate the key with <code>setIsStrongBoxBacked(true)</code> and an attestation challenge issued by the server, then verify the certificate chain on the server up to the Google hardware attestation root and check <code>attestationSecurityLevel</code>. Derive the alias per-installation.</p></div>
+  </div>
+</article>
