+<article class="vuln card" id="STOR-16" data-sev="Low" data-cat="Attack surface" data-search="stor-16 small exported surface, but the launcher activity is world-reachable attack surface the manifest exports exactly three components: com.nivaroid.topfollow.ui.topactivity (main/launcher, no permission), com.google.firebase.iid.firebaseinstanceidreceiver (protected by com.google.android.c2dm.permission.send ) and androidx.profileinstaller.profileinstallreceiver (protected by android.permission.dump ). there are no deep links, no content providers beyond the firebase/androidx-startup initialisers, and no exported services; taskactionreceiver and dotasksservice are not exported. the direct ipc attack surface is small a genuinely positive finding but the two firebase/androidx receivers are framework components whose protections are only as good as the platform's, and the launcher activity is the world-reachable entry point to the whole credential flow. out/07_androidmanifest.xml ; uses-permission set is internet, foreground_service(_special_use), post_notifications, access_network_state, wake_lock, c2dm.receive plus a self-declared signature-level dynamic_receiver_not_exported_permission keep the receiver and service unexported, and validate every intent extra on the launcher path since it is world-reachable.">
+  <header>
+    <div class="vid">STOR-16</div>
+    <span class="badge" style="--c:#4dd7ff">Low</span>
+    <span class="cat">Attack surface</span>
+  </header>
+  <h3>Small exported surface, but the launcher activity is world-reachable</h3>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div><h4>How it works</h4><p>The manifest exports exactly three components: <code>com.nivaroid.topfollow.ui.TopActivity</code> (MAIN/LAUNCHER, no permission), <code>com.google.firebase.iid.FirebaseInstanceIdReceiver</code> (protected by <code>com.google.android.c2dm.permission.SEND</code>) and <code>androidx.profileinstaller.ProfileInstallReceiver</code> (protected by <code>android.permission.DUMP</code>). There are no deep links, no content providers beyond the Firebase/androidx-startup initialisers, and no exported services; <code>TaskActionReceiver</code> and <code>DoTasksService</code> are not exported.</p></div>
+    <div><h4>Impact</h4><p>The direct IPC attack surface is small &mdash; a genuinely positive finding &mdash; but the two Firebase/androidx receivers are framework components whose protections are only as good as the platform's, and the launcher activity is the world-reachable entry point to the whole credential flow.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h4>Evidence</h4>
+  <p class="ev"><code>out/07_AndroidManifest.xml</code>; <code>uses-permission</code> set is INTERNET, FOREGROUND_SERVICE(_SPECIAL_USE), POST_NOTIFICATIONS, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, WAKE_LOCK, c2dm.RECEIVE plus a self-declared signature-level <code>DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION</code></p>
+  <div class="rowend">
+    <div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><div class="chips"><a class="chip" href="#script-01_anti_tamper_killer">01_anti_tamper_killer.js</a></div></div>
+    <div class="fixcol"><h4>Remediation</h4><p>Keep the receiver and service unexported, and validate every intent extra on the launcher path since it is world-reachable.</p></div>
+  </div>
+</article>
+</div>
